> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Pacing is three dials and one writing habit:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 28]{/color} — milliseconds between revealed characters.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[autodelay 1650]{/color} — auto-play pause after each line.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 600]{/color} — a hard pause everyone feels.
narrator: Keep most dialogue lines short enough to read in one comfortable breath.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Default recipe: textspeed 28, autodelay 1200-1800, wait only for intentional beats.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_textspeed
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Text Speed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_textspeed
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[textspeed N]{/b} sets milliseconds between each character appearing on screen.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 28]{/color}
narrator: Lower = faster. Higher = slower. The value persists until you change it.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Feel the difference:
[textspeed 12]
Lavender: Textspeed 12 — almost instant. Action scenes, rapid banter, impatient characters.
[textspeed 28]
Lavender: Textspeed 28 — the default. Comfortable for most dialogue.
[textspeed 50]
Lavender: Textspeed 50 — slow and weighty. Dramatic reveals, final words, narrated dread.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Change speed mid-scene to match the energy. A confrontation might start at 28 and drop to 40 for the final line.
narrator: {b}Typical range:{/b} 12–20 fast, 22–35 normal, 38–55 slow.
> Next: autodelay -> section_autodelay
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Auto-Delay ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_autodelay
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[autodelay N]{/b} controls how long the engine waits after a line finishes before advancing in auto-play mode.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[autodelay 1650]{/color}
narrator: Only affects auto-play. Manual clicking ignores this value.
[show lavender center talking]
[autodelay 600]
Lavender: Autodelay 600 — barely a breath between lines. Feels rushed.
[autodelay 2800]
Lavender: Autodelay 2800 — a long, contemplative pause. Each line sits.
[autodelay 1650]
Lavender: Autodelay 1650 — the default. A comfortable reading gap.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Match autodelay to the weight of the scene. A fast conversation wants 800–1200. A eulogy wants 2000+.
> Next: wait -> section_waits
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Wait ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_waits
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[wait N]{/b} freezes the scene for N milliseconds. It affects {i}everyone{/i} — manual clickers included.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 800]{/color}
Lavender: Three...
[wait 400]
Lavender: Two...
[wait 400]
Lavender: One.
[wait 800]
narrator: ...
[wait 600]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That silence was four [wait] commands. 400ms, 400ms, 800ms, 600ms.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: When to use [wait]:
narrator: {b}Before a reveal{/b} — build anticipation with 400–800ms of nothing.
narrator: {b}After a shock{/b} — let the moment sit before the next line.
narrator: {b}During hesitation{/b} — a character pauses mid-thought.
narrator: {b}Between actions{/b} — [hide], [wait 500], [show] feels like real movement.
Lavender: Keep waits under 1200ms in most cases. Longer pauses test the player's patience.
> Next: line length -> section_line_length
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Line Length ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_line_length
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: No command for this one — it's a writing technique. Compare:
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Run.
Lavender: Now.
Lavender: Don't look back.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: She sat by the window for a long time, watching the fog erase the hills one ridge at a time, and thought about everything she hadn't said.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Short lines are fast, urgent, punchy. Long lines are slow, contemplative, immersive.
narrator: {b}Rule of thumb:{/b} two sentences max per dialogue line. If it wraps in the text box, consider splitting it.
Lavender: Vary line length deliberately. Ten short lines feel breathless. Five long lines feel heavy. Alternate.
> Next: all tools combined -> section_rhythm
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Live Demo ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_rhythm
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Watch how textspeed, wait, and line length work together. I'll annotate after.
[wait 400]
[textspeed 20]
narrator: The hallway was empty.
[wait 300]
[show lavender right talking]
[textspeed 40]
Lavender: Hello?
[wait 700]
[textspeed 18]
narrator: No answer.
[wait 400]
[show lavender center emphasis]
[textspeed 35]
Lavender: {i}I swear I heard something.{/i}
[wait 600]
[textspeed 16]
narrator: A door at the end of the hall creaked open — just an inch.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's what drove that scene:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 20]{/color} — narrator line appears quickly, setting up the space.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 40]{/color} — "Hello?" crawls out. Hesitation is in the speed.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 700]{/color} — long silence after the question. No one answers.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 16]{/color} — the final narrator line snaps in fast. The door moves before you process it.
narrator: Line lengths shrank as tension rose: 5 words, 1 word, 2 words, 6 words, then a longer payoff.
> Practice: pace a scene -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I'll play the same three lines with different pacing. Your job: identify which tools changed.

@label practice_round_1
Lavender: Round 1 — fast:
[textspeed 12]
narrator: She opened the letter.
Lavender: It's from him.
narrator: Her hands were steady.
[textspeed 28]
Lavender: Round 2 — slow:
[textspeed 42]
narrator: She opened the letter.
[wait 500]
Lavender: It's from him.
[wait 600]
narrator: Her hands were steady.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Same words. Completely different scene. The fast version felt routine. The slow version felt heavy.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: What changed:
narrator: {b}Round 1:{/b} textspeed 12, no waits. Brisk, unemotional, informational.
narrator: {b}Round 2:{/b} textspeed 42, [wait 500] and [wait 600] between lines. Loaded with dread.
Lavender: Now try it yourself — open this file and change the numbers. Move the waits. Add a [wait 1000] after "It's from him." and feel what happens.
> Replay the demo -> practice_round_1
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

